traceloop (now called bead-calculus) is a bead-running puzzle game where you build little marble-machine circuits out of wires, gates, and loops, then drop “beads” (signals) into the contraption and watch the causal choreography unfold. The core verb is routing: you splice tracks, add forks/joins, and introduce feedback loops that behave like traced wiring diagrams—beads can carry payloads, split, recombine, or get delayed, and the “win condition” is usually “make this invariant true” rather than “reach a single goal state.” It’s half toy, half microscope: a cute kinetic sandbox that quietly teaches decomposition, factoring, and what changes (and what must not) when you cut a system at arbitrary boundaries.

