String diagrams are basically marble machines for algebra. So if your game is already “beads move on tracks,” you’re 90% of the way to a diagrammatic language for (traced) monoidal categories—without ever saying that out loud in the tutorial.

Here’s the clean mapping:
	•	Wires = types / resources. A track segment is a wire labeled by a bead-kind (bit, token, color, “charge”…).
	•	Boxes = primitive operations. A gate that flips a bead color, delays it, duplicates it, etc. That’s a morphism f: A \to B.
	•	Sequential composition = plug output track into input track. That’s g \circ f.
	•	Parallel composition = put machines side-by-side. That’s the tensor f \otimes g.
	•	Feedback loop = trace. A literal loop-back track implements \mathrm{Tr}^X(f): A \to B from f: A \otimes X \to B \otimes X.

That alone gets you string diagrams for traced symmetric monoidal categories as the level editor.

Make it gamey: string-diagram rewrite moves as “mechanical equivalences”

The killer feature is that string diagrams have an equational theory that corresponds to “obviously the machine still works if I…” moves. Turn those into puzzle mechanics:
	•	Interchange law (sliding independent boxes past each other): if two gadgets are on disjoint tracks, you can commute them. In the game: you can physically slide modules along straight tracks when nothing interacts.
	•	Yanking (cups/caps straightening): if you have a bend that just creates a loop with no work inside, it cancels. In the game: certain loop fixtures can be “pulled taut” to delete a useless loop.
	•	Trace sliding (dinaturality): you can move a box in/out of a feedback loop in specific ways. In the game: you can “lift” a module across a loop junction when its interface matches, like moving a pulley across a belt.

Now the player is proving diagram equalities by doing physical refactors.

Add cups/caps: bead-pair creation and annihilation (compact-closed vibes)

If you want full-on string-diagram juice, introduce a gadget that spawns paired beads—think “magnetic” bead pairs:
	•	Cup: creates a correlated pair \eta: I \to A^\* \otimes A (a bead and an anti-bead, or two linked colors).
	•	Cap: annihilates a matching pair \epsilon: A \otimes A^\* \to I.

Mechanically: a cup is a little feeder that emits two linked beads onto two tracks; a cap is a collector that consumes them only if they match/link. This gives you:
	•	Bidirectional wiring tricks
	•	Teleport-y refactors (“bend a wire around the back of the board” becomes legal and meaningful)
	•	A direct bridge to the Int-construction intuition you were riffing on earlier: forward wires and backward “obligations” become literal bead streams.

Beads as “tokens in a token machine” (GoI / interaction nets energy)

If you want it to feel deep rather than decorative: let beads be the operational semantics.
	•	A diagram is a static object.
	•	A bead run is an execution trace.
	•	Certain diagram equalities correspond to observational equivalence: “for all bead inputs, the outputs match.”

Then puzzles can be:
	•	Implement a behavior (compile a truth table / routing spec) with a limited palette of generators.
	•	Optimize a machine: reduce latency, number of modules, or energy loss—while preserving behavior (i.e., show equivalence via rewrite moves + simulation tests).
	•	Separate two machines: find an input bead pattern that distinguishes them (counterexample hunting as gameplay).

That’s basically “program synthesis + equational reasoning” disguised as a toy.

A small “algebra of bead gadgets” to start with

You don’t need many primitives; you want a presented category: generators + equations.

Good starting generators (all very physical):
	•	Swap / crossing (symmetric monoidal structure)
	•	Delay (a buffer; makes time observable)
	•	Merge / split (careful: true copying is special—if you model linear resources, copying is restricted; if you model classical data, add a comonoid)
	•	Join (a “detector” that fires only when two beads arrive together → synchronization)
	•	Trace junction (loop-back port)
	•	Optional: Cup/Cap (paired beads)

If you include split/merge, you can choose your flavor:
	•	If split is free, you’re in “classical signal” land (cartesian-ish).
	•	If split costs something or requires a special “duplicator” module, you’re in “linear logic / resource” land (more interesting for puzzles, honestly).

UI idea that makes string diagrams explicit

Let the editor have a “diagram view” toggle:
	•	Machine view: cute parts, gravity, beads rolling.
	•	Diagram view: the exact same thing, but flattened into a string diagram with labeled wires and ports, and your rewrite moves become clean diagram rewrites.

Players will slowly realize they’re doing algebra when they thought they were doing tinkertoys. Delicious.

Net effect: your bead machine isn’t inspired by string diagrams—it is a string diagram whose operational semantics is “beads moving,” and whose proof theory is “refactor the machine without changing what it computes.” The universe loves reusing the same idea with different costumes.

