Yes. “Rewrite-to-canonical” puzzles are the secret boss fight of programming languages, except you get to make it tactile and kind instead of a 40-page paper that ends with “left to the reader.”

bead-calculus is a banger name, and your “QED as a win screen” framing is exactly right. Here’s how to make that equational layer actually work (and not collapse into either “anything goes” or “too hard to tell if I’m right”).

The core trick: treat every level as living in a presented theory: generators + equations. Your gadgets are generators (boxes, swaps, delays, split/join, trace junction, maybe cups/caps later). Your rewrite moves are the equations, but embodied as mechanical transformations. Then:
	•	Simulation (“beads as oracle”) gives behavioral confidence: you didn’t break what it does.
	•	Rewrite steps (“moves as proof”) give structural legitimacy: you transformed it using only sanctioned equalities.

That lets you separate “works” from “provably the same thing.”

Make “canonical form” real: choose a normalization strategy

Pick a canonicalization scheme that your moves obviously support. Example: a left-to-right normal form.

Working theory (simple, powerful, implementable tonight):
	1.	Everything flows left → right across “time columns”.
	2.	Swaps bubble outward: crossings are pushed to standardized “swap layers” between columns (think bubble-sort for wires).
	3.	Traces are explicit: every feedback loop is represented by a dedicated “trace portal” module, and you normalize by sliding eligible stuff out of the loop mouth.

Then your three moves map cleanly:
	•	Slide (interchange) is your commuting conversion: it’s the justification for bubble-sorting independent boxes into a canonical order.
	•	Straighten (yanking) is your loop identity elimination: it deletes bureaucratic plumbing.
	•	Thread (trace sliding) is dinaturality in a trenchcoat: it’s what makes traced wiring diagrams feel like refactoring rather than teleportation.

If you implement only those, you’re basically building a normalization-by-rewrites engine with a bead-based regression test harness.

How you prevent “rewrite chaos”

You’ll want rewrites that are sound (never change meaning) and locally checkable (you don’t need global reasoning to see they apply). The game feel depends on this.

Concretely: each module has typed ports (“red bead track”, “clock bead”, etc.). A rewrite is allowed iff:
	•	the port types match up,
	•	the rewritten subgraph has the same boundary interface (same external ports),
	•	and (optional but juicy) the move preserves a local “energy” or “latency” measure unless it’s explicitly a “simplify” move like yanking.

This makes every move feel like a legal chess move, not a vibe.

Puzzle generation that won’t kill you

Tonight’s jam-friendly loop:
	1.	Start from a canonical machine C.
	2.	Apply random inverse rewrites to scramble it into A (or into B).
	3.	Give the player A and target C (or A\to B), plus a limited move set.
	4.	Use bead runs as a sanity check / hint system, not as the proof.

This guarantees solvability and lets you tune difficulty by the scramble depth and which inverses you permit.

One extra mechanic that makes it transcendent

Let the player pin an invariant and watch it glow.

Example invariants that are both intuitive and “string-diagrammy”:
	•	bead count conservation on certain wire types (linear resources),
	•	“no simultaneous arrival” (synchronization constraint),
	•	periodicity / frequency (delay loops become oscillators),
	•	“phase” labels that must match at joins (forces lawful trace use).

Then your rewrites become conservation-preserving surgeries on the machine. That’s your “half microscope” vibe: you’re not just solving; you’re learning what survives transformation.

If you want a tight north star for the jam: implement a tiny port-graph representation + pattern-matching rewrites + bead simulation on top, then ship one level that teaches Slide → Straighten → Thread and ends with the first QED stamp. That single level will already feel like sorcery.
